    12	# Design analysis for the D9 variant
    13	# ----------------------------------
    14	# spec:
    15	#   concretization:
    16	#     - The legend stays unchanged: four colored bars in a grey top band still encode only left/right turns.
    17	#     - The source is fixed to a solid teal 2x2 square so the initial ribbon width is always 2.
    18	#     - A beam is still modeled as a moving square footprint, but the footprint size is now dynamic.
    19	#       Straight segments therefore appear as 1/2/3/4-cell-wide ribbons depending on prior turns.
    20	#     - Width update rule: after a turn, inspect the straight segment that just ended.
    21	#       If that segment moved at least 7 cells, the next segment widens by 1; otherwise it narrows by 1.
    22	#       Width never drops below 1.
    23	#   behavior_trace:
    24	#     - Original behavior near a north obstacle:
    25	#         source width = 2, beam moves up, turns, and continues sideways with width still 2.
    26	#     - Variant behavior:
    27	#         source width = 2, beam moves up for len=8, hits obstacle, turns, and the sideways continuation
    28	#         is width 3; if len=4 instead, that continuation is width 1.
    29	#     - The changed width matters twice: visually in the painted ribbon and logically when a turned beam
    30	#       reaches a second obstacle, because the obstacle span it can hit now depends on the new width.
    31	#   complexity_calibration:
    32	#     - Only two width outcomes appear after the first turn (1 or 3), and with at most two turns per beam
    33	#       the maximum width is 4. This keeps the rule visible without making tracing combinatorially messy.
    34	#     - Generator samples are forced to contain at least one short first segment (<=4) and one long first
    35	#       segment (>=7), so shrinking and widening are both demonstrated repeatedly.
    36	#   implicit_assumptions_review:
    37	#     - The original solver assumes one immutable square size `sz` for bounds, obstacle tests, painting,
    38	#       cycle detection, and second-hit geometry. Those assumptions are invalid once width changes.
    39	#     - The new solver therefore tracks `(r, c, direction, width)` and resets `segment_len` after every turn.
    40	#     - Keeping the top-left anchor fixed at a bend is a new geometric convention; generator constraints are
    41	#       computed with exactly the same convention so solver and generator agree on every corner.
    42	#   placement_legality:
    43	#     - Obstacles remain edge-touching solid rectangles and never overlap each other or the source.
    44	#     - The generator first decides which side can chain into a second hit, then selects obstacle spans that
    45	#       must cover required incoming lanes and must avoid excluded lanes for all other turned beams.
    46	#     - Every accepted sample is re-solved by `main()` to confirm the constructed input is legal.
    47	#   observability_and_cleanliness:
    48	#     - No invisible property such as color id controls width; only visible straight-segment length does.
    49	#     - Width changes are visible as noticeably thinner/thicker post-turn ribbons rather than tiny markers.
    50	#     - Legend, source, and obstacles keep the original visual vocabulary, so the only new thing to infer is
    51	#       "long segment widens next ribbon, short segment narrows it."
    52	#   color_change_reasoning:
    53	#     - No color remapping is introduced. Colors still serve only as obstacle identities tied to legend turns.
    54	
    55	
    56	_DIRS = [(-1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1)]
    57	_N, _E, _S, _W = range(4)
    58	
    59	LEGEND_COLOR_POOL = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]
    60	BG_COLOR_POOL = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 9]
    61	LEGEND_BG = 5
    62	TEAL = 8
    63	
    64	GRID_H_RANGE = (19, 24)
    65	GRID_W = 24
    66	LEGEND_H = 6
    67	LEGEND_CELL_STARTS = [1, 7, 13, 19]
